WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.756
Your naive solution recomputes the same answer millions of times and times out. Dynamic programming saves each answer once and reuses it.

00:00:11.956 --> 00:00:12.885
Pause and think.

00:00:13.285 --> 00:00:16.327
What two signs tell you a problem is dynamic programming?

00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:24.041
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.771
Dynamic programming sounds fancy, but it just means: do not solve the same small problem twice.

00:00:33.121 --> 00:00:37.579
Imagine climbing a staircase and writing the answer to each step on a sticky note.

00:00:38.029 --> 00:00:44.090
To answer step ten, you read the notes on steps nine and eight, instead of climbing from the bottom again.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.862
Those sticky notes are the DP table.

00:00:46.867 --> 00:01:01.170
New word: D P. Dynamic Programming: solve a problem by storing answers to smaller sub-problems and reusing them; also DisplayPort. Picture it like this: Writing intermediate sums in a notebook so you never redo them.

00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:06.074
Use DP when two things are true.

00:01:06.524 --> 00:01:13.861
First, the big answer can be built from answers to smaller versions of the same question. That is called optimal substructure.

00:01:14.311 --> 00:01:20.430
Second, the same smaller questions come up again and again. That is called overlapping subproblems.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.082
Then pick a state, write the recurrence, and fill a table from small to big.

00:01:26.432 --> 00:01:32.806
Let us solve coin change: make six using coins one, three, and four, with the fewest coins.

00:01:33.256 --> 00:01:39.491
Greedy would grab four, then one, then one: three coins. But three plus three is only two coins!

00:01:39.941 --> 00:01:44.237
The DP table stores the best answer for each amount from zero to six.

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:50.468
For each amount a, try each coin as the last coin: one plus dp of a minus that coin.

00:01:50.918 --> 00:01:58.360
Watch the table fill. dp of six is the minimum of dp five, dp three, and dp two, plus one. That is two.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.046
There are two styles.

00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:06.290
Top down: write the recursion, then add a cache, called memoization.

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:17.513
New word: cache. A small fast store of recently used data so you don't fetch it from the slow place again. Picture it like this: Keeping the salt next to the stove instead of in the basement.

00:02:17.913 --> 00:02:27.804
New word: memoization. Caching a function's answers so repeated calls are instant. Picture it like this: Writing answers in the margin so you never redo the sum.

00:02:28.204 --> 00:02:37.957
New word: recursion. A function that solves a problem by calling itself on smaller pieces. Picture it like this: Russian dolls: open one to find a smaller one.

00:02:39.607 --> 00:02:43.915
Bottom up: fill an array in order, with no recursion and no stack risk.

00:02:44.073 --> 00:02:53.140
New word: stack. Last-in-first-out structure; also the memory area holding function calls. Picture it like this: A stack of plates.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:03:01.981
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:03:03.623 --> 00:03:08.278
Both have the same complexity. Here, amount times number of coins.

00:03:09.628 --> 00:03:12.090
Counting bits is DP in disguise.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:18.345
The number of one bits in i equals the count for i shifted right by one, plus the lowest bit.

00:03:18.795 --> 00:03:23.578
Shifting right just drops the last bit, and we already know that smaller answer.

00:03:24.028 --> 00:03:26.919
So each entry is one lookup and one addition.

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:35.455
The hardest part of DP is naming the state. Practice coin change and counting bits, then you will see the pattern everywhere.

00:03:35.431 --> 00:03:36.685
Back to the question.

00:03:37.085 --> 00:03:40.127
What two signs tell you a problem is dynamic programming?

00:03:40.527 --> 00:03:45.194
Overlapping sub-problems and an optimal answer built from optimal smaller answers.

00:03:46.394 --> 00:03:47.265
Quick recap.

00:03:47.665 --> 00:03:49.987
Write down answers to small problems.

00:03:50.387 --> 00:03:52.465
Define the state and the transition.

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:55.245
Top-down memo or bottom-up table.
